# Changelog

## 1.1.3 — `C41` (the 22uF LDO output bulk) is reconnected to `+3V3`
**Schematic-only revision.** `32bit-5v.kicad_sch` gains **one wire segment and one
junction** (16 added lines, 0 deleted); `32bit-5v.kicad_pcb` is **byte-identical** to
1.1.2 (same SHA-256 `0294006d…`) — **no copper was added**, because the board never lost
the connection. This closes the pre-existing `1244ad0` defect that 1.1.2 found and
deliberately did not fix (`POWER.md` §5, §6 item 8).

* **Fix `C41` (22 µF LDO output bulk) — its `+3V3` connection was missing since
  `1244ad0`; one segment + junction added; DRC connectivity now clean.**
* **The defect.** Commit `1244ad0` re-placed `C41` from `(525.78, 29.21)` to
  `(464.82, 33.02)` and left its top stub wire `(464.82, 29.21) → (464.82, 26.67)`
  ending in mid-air: the `+3V3` run `(459.74, 24.13) → (467.36, 24.13)` passes **2.54 mm
  above** it and never touches. The netlist therefore carried the auto net
  `unconnected-(C41-Pad1)`, and ERC carried `+1 error pin_not_connected (Symbol C41
  Pin 1)` plus `+1 warning unconnected_wire_endpoint (Vertical Wire, 0.0254 mm)`.
* **The fix** — exactly the remedy scoped in `POWER.md` §5: one 2.54 mm segment, one
  junction, no part moved, nothing else touched.
  ```
  (wire     (pts (xy 464.82 26.67) (xy 464.82 24.13))  ; closes the 2.54 mm gap
  (junction (at 464.82 24.13))                          ; T onto the +3V3 run
  ```
* **Measured before → after** (KiCad 10.0.6 `kicad-cli`; invocations as in
  `verification/README.md`):
  * **netlist** — 77 → **76 nets**: the auto net `unconnected-(C41-Pad1)` is gone and
    `+3V3` gains exactly `('C41','1')` (9 → 10 nodes). `+3V3` is the **only** net whose
    node set changed — the other 75 shared nets are node-for-node identical, so the new
    junction merges nothing and shorts nothing. No net was renamed, merged or split.
  * **ERC `--severity-all`** — 56 → **54** (35/21 → **34/20**). Normalised (severity +
    type + description + item positions) against the 1.1.2 source the diff is **exactly
    two findings removed and none added**: the `C41` `pin_not_connected` error and the
    `C41` `unconnected_wire_endpoint` warning, both at `(464.82, 29.21)` = `C41` pin 1.
    The 1.1.3 report is in fact **element-for-element identical to the report shipped
    with v1.1.1** (`verification/erc-32bit-5v-1.1.1-pre-1244ad0.json`) — the defect and
    both of its artefacts are fully gone. Note this does **not** restore
    `check_ldo_swap_netlist.py`: that 19/19 proof is bound to the frozen
    1.1.0 → 1.1.1-as-released pair (unchanged by this revision).
  * **Board DRC `--schematic-parity`** — **105 violations / 0 unconnected items, both
    before and after** (the board is untouched), and schematic parity **19 → 18**. The
    single removed item is exactly `Pad net (+3V3) doesn't match net given by schematic
    (unconnected-(C41-Pad1))`, i.e. the board and the schematic now agree on `C41`'s net
    for the first time since `1244ad0`. The other 18 parity items are the inherited ones
    (`U5` footprint/value/LCSC, the never-placed `C42`, the `J5` B-row pins and the five
    `C9`..`C13`) and remain the owner's routing-pass scope, not this revision's work.
  * **`C41` on the board was never broken.** Pad 1 is `+3V3` at `(66.8, 59.85)` on
    B.Cu, reached by the 0.8 mm track `(64.05, 60.4) → (66.8, 60.4)`; `pcbnew`
    connectivity fits the pad into the `+3V3` cluster and DRC reports **0 unconnected
    items**. There is no `+3V3` zone/pour anywhere on this board (the only pour is `GND`
    on `In1.Cu`) and no dead `+3V3` stub near `C41`, so **nothing was added and nothing
    was cleaned up on the copper**: the defect was schematic-only — i.e. it lived in the
    netlist that a board re-sync would have been built from, not in the 1.1.0 layout.
* **Not touched**: `32bit-5v.kicad_pcb` (byte-identical to 1.1.2), `32bit-5v.kicad_pro`,
  `production/`, `code/`, and the **`verification/` fixture set**. Those fixtures are the
  **1.1.2** side of the frozen trim proof — `check_bulk_cap_trim.py` consumes
  `netlist-32bit-5v.kicadsexpr` and `erc-32bit-5v.json` as its current-revision inputs —
  so overwriting them with 1.1.3 output would invalidate the 20/20 proof. They stay
  exactly as they are, the same way the 1.1.0 and 1.1.1-as-released netlists stayed
  frozen in 1.1.2.
* `POWER.md` carries a **v1.1.3 note** at the top: its `C41` statements (§1 rail map,
  §1.1, §1.2, §5, §6 item 8) are written against the **v1.1.2** source and are annotated
  where they would otherwise still read as an open defect.

## 1.1.2 — ESP32 rail: the five legacy 100uF bulk caps (`C9`..`C13`) are removed
**Schematic-only revision.** `32bit-5v.kicad_sch` changes; `32bit-5v.kicad_pcb` is
deliberately **not** touched (no placement, no routing, no zone refill, no footprint
deletion) — the owner does the routing pass, which now also has to delete the five
orphaned 1206 bulk-cap footprints. The `production/` BOM/CPL exports stay stale for the
same reason (they are generated from the board). See `POWER.md` §1.3 for the full
rationale and §6 for the hand-off table.

* **Removed `C9`, `C10`, `C11`, `C12`, `C13`** — five generic **100uF 1206** capacitors
  that sat in parallel on `+3V3_ESP32`, i.e. behind the `FB1` ferrite, directly across
  the ESP32 module's supply. Together with the 10uF the rail carried ≈**510uF**; it now
  carries **10.1uF**. The bank dates from the pre-regulator era (0.3.0, *"use better
  power supply for esp32 side with ferrite beads and caps"*), when the 3.3V rail was an
  unregulated feed and brute-force bulk was the only mitigation available. Since 1.1.0
  the module has its own 600mA LDO, so the bank was sized against a premise that no
  longer holds.
* **Kept, unchanged in value/footprint/fields**: `C1` 0.1uF 0402 and `C2` 10uF 0603 on
  `+3V3_ESP32`, `FB1` (GZ2012D121TF) and the `R3`/`C3` EN delay. The only change on the
  rail is the deletion of the five 100uF parts plus their 20 stub/bus wires and 8
  junctions, so nothing dangles.
* **What the ESP32 specifies** (Espressif *ESP32-S3 Hardware Design Guidelines*,
  schematic checklist; `ESP32-S3-WROOM-1` datasheet "Power Supply"): a **10uF + 0.1uF**
  pair at the module's 3V3 input — which is exactly the `+3V3_ESP32` rail here and is
  already `C2` + `C1` — an **LC circuit on the VDD3P3 rail with the inductor rated
  ≥500mA** (`FB1`, already fitted), and **≥10uF at the main power entrance** (`C40`
  10uF + `C42` 0.1uF on `+5V`). The five 100uF parts were an addition on top of that
  specification, not a requirement of it. The guidelines' warning about *excessively
  large* capacitors is about the **chip-level `VDD_SPI` rail** (internal to the module
  here), so it is quoted for context only and is **not** the reason for this trim — see
  `POWER.md` §1.3 for the table that separates the two levels.
* **Margin is reduced deliberately, and the replacement is not validated.** A 500uF bank
  also supplied charge for slow load transients; 10uF does not, even when both "meet the
  datasheet". This revision contains **no measurement** of load-transient/brownout
  behaviour on the trimmed rail, and no measurement of `C2`'s DC-bias-reduced effective
  capacitance. Both are **pending bench items** (`POWER.md` §1.3 and §5); if they show
  brownout or PSRAM-related resets, the remedy is **one** 0805/1206 bulk cap back behind
  `FB1`, not the bank.
* **Net effect** (proved in `verification/proof-bulk-cap-trim.json`, 20/20): `+3V3_ESP32`
  = {`C1`.1, `C2`.1, `FB1`.1, `U1`.2}, `GND` lost only the five pin-2 nodes, every other
  net in the design is byte-identical, and no surviving component's value, footprint or
  fields changed. 77 nets before and after; no net was merged, split or renamed.
* **Verification** (KiCad 10.0.6): `sch erc --severity-all` stays at **56 (35 error /
  21 warning)** and — compared as normalised findings (severity + type + affected items
  + positions, not just the total) — the before and after reports are **identical**, so
  0 new and 0 changed findings. The new trim proof is **20/20** and chains
  1.1.0 → 1.1.1-as-released → 1.1.1 source → 1.1.2 link by link. The regulator-swap
  proof still reproduces **19/19** from the frozen artefacts.
* **Fixture correction (worth recording).** The `verification/` artefacts shipped with
  1.1.1 were **stale**: commit `1244ad0` ("Update the 32bit schematics a bit") changed
  the schematic *after* they were generated, which is why the v1.1.1 note claimed 54
  ERC findings while the same source actually produces 56. This revision freezes a
  correct 1.1.1 baseline (`erc-32bit-5v-1.1.1.json`,
  `netlist-32bit-5v-1.1.1.kicadsexpr`), keeps the shipped report for the record
  (`erc-32bit-5v-1.1.1-pre-1244ad0.json`), and explains the two-finding difference.
* **Found, NOT fixed — `C41` is disconnected (pre-existing, `1244ad0`).** That commit
  re-placed `C41` (22uF, the LDO output bulk) and left its `+3V3` stub ending in
  mid-air, so the netlist now contains `unconnected-(C41-Pad1)` and ERC carries exactly
  `+1 error pin_not_connected (C41 pin 1)` and `+1 warning unconnected_wire_endpoint
  (Vertical Wire, length 0.0254 mm)` versus the shipped 1.1.1 report. It is **not** part
  of this revision and is **not** repaired here — it is logged in `POWER.md` §5 (with
  the exact one-wire + one-junction remedy) and §6 item 8, and is modelled as its own
  link in the chain proof so the history stays exact. **Do not read it as harmless:**
  `C8`/`C6` keep the 3.3V rail decoupled so the module is expected to run, but the
  regulator is missing 22uF of its documented output bulk and that has not been
  validated. It should be fixed **before board synchronisation or manufacture**. Note
  also that fixing `C41` does not restore `check_ldo_swap_netlist.py` against a 1.1.2
  netlist — that proof belongs to the frozen 1.1.0 → 1.1.1-as-released pair.
* **Board-side consequences (owner's routing pass)**: the board still carries `C9`..`C13`
  footprints. Parity (`kicad-cli pcb drc --schematic-parity`) has **not been re-run**
  since the v1.1.0 board run, so this is an expectation rather than a result: it should
  report at least `U5` (wrong footprint, from 1.1.1), `C42` (in the schematic since
  1.1.1, never placed), `C41` (net mismatch — see above) and the five `C9`..`C13`
  capacitors still on the board. The `production/` exports remain the 1.1.0 ones.
  Removing the five 1206 parts frees real estate exactly where `C1`/`C2` need to sit —
  with no distant reservoir left, their **placement close to `U1` pin 2** is what
  carries the decoupling (`POWER.md` §1.3, §6 items 2 and 6).
* **Not touched**: `32bit-5v.kicad_pcb`, `32bit-5v.kicad_pro`, `production/`, `code/`,
  and the module's outline/connectors/signal routing.

## 1.1.1 — same 3.3V regulator as the base module (`AP2112K-3.3`), schematic only
**Schematic-only revision.** `32bit-5v.kicad_sch` changes; `32bit-5v.kicad_pcb`
is deliberately **not** touched (no placement, no routing, no zone refill, no
footprint deletion) — the owner does the routing pass. Consequently
`kicad-cli pcb drc --schematic-parity` now reports the regulator mismatch (the
`schematic` has `U5` = SOT-23-5 while the board still carries the old SOT-223
footprint): that is **expected and owned by the owner's routing pass**, not a
defect to fix in this revision. The `production/` BOM/CPL exports are also left
stale for the same reason (they are generated by the JLCPCB toolkit from the
board, which did not change; regenerate them after the routing pass).

* **`U5` is now the base module's regulator: `AP2112K-3.3`** (Diodes, SOT-23-5,
  LCSC `C51118`, MPN `AP2112K-3.3TRG1`, 600mA, 250mV dropout). The Value,
  Footprint, Datasheet, Description, LCSC, MPN and Manufacturer fields are copied
  verbatim from the base's `U5` so both boards order the identical part
  (machine-checked). The `AP7361C-33E-13` (SOT-223) symbol, instance and its
  embedded library symbol are gone — the string `AP7361C` appears nowhere in the
  schematic or the exported netlist.
* **Pinout/net effect**: `VIN` = 1, `EN` = 3, `GND` = 2, `NC` = 4 (hidden
  no-connect pin, exactly as in the base), `VOUT` = 5. `+3V3` still drives every
  load it drove in 1.1.0 (`C6`, `C8`, `FB1`, `R10`, `R12`, `R15`, `R3`,
  `U3` pin 8, `C41`) — only the regulator's own pin moved (old `VO` 3 → new
  `VOUT` 5). `+3V3_ESP32` and `+3V3_8388` are unchanged, node for node.
* **`+5V` is still confined to the regulator path**: {`V_SUPPLY1`.1-5, `U5` VIN,
  `U5` EN, `C40`.1, `C42`.1} — nothing else on the board sees 5V.
* **EN handling**: EN is wired **directly to VIN** on `+5V`, so the 3.3V rail is
  permanently enabled. The base brings EN out to `R4` 100k + `C16` 220nF + `SW1`
  (its user switch for the base's own 3.3V rail); that is deliberately not
  reproduced — the module must always be on and no switch/divider is wanted.
* **Decoupling mirrors the base's scheme** (base §1: input bulk + 0.1uF, output
  cap(s)):
  * **new `C42`** = 0.1uF/16V X7R **0402**, LCSC `C1525` / `CL05B104KO5NNNC` —
    the base's own 0.1uF part (`C1`/`C21`), added as the regulator input HF cap
    (schematic at the `+5V` node next to `C40`);
  * **`C40` kept** = 10uF/25V X5R 0805 (`C15850`, Samsung CL21A106KAYNNNE): the
    base fits 1uF (`C2`) at the pin but has 22uF + 0.1uF upstream of its input
    ferrite; the module's 5V arrives on a slot socket with no upstream bulk, so the
    larger value is kept (same Samsung CL21 0805 family as the base's caps);
  * **`C41` kept** = 22uF/25V X5R 0805 (`C45783`, Samsung CL21A226MAQNNNE): the
    base fits 4.7uF (`C4`) at the pin then 22uF after the ferrite; the module keeps
    its larger bulk local to the regulator for the ESP32 + ES8388 load (same
    Samsung CL21 family);
  * `C6` 0.1uF / `C8` 10uF on `+3V3` and everything downstream of `FB1`/`R15` are
    unchanged.
* **Verification** (KiCad 10.0.6): `sch erc --severity-all` 54 → **54**
  (34 error / 20 warning), **no new finding and no new finding kind**;
  netlist acceptance `verification/netlist-proof-ldo-swap.json` = **19/19 pass**
  (the `+5V`/`+3V3`/`+3V3_ESP32`/`+3V3_8388`/`GND` node sets above, no `AP7361C`
  anywhere, and `U5`'s Value/Footprint/Datasheet/Description/LCSC/MPN/Manufacturer
  identical to the base's `U5`). Board artefacts (DRC, parity) are left as the
  1.1.0 runs.
* **Headroom**: owner-confirmed that **WiFi/BT is never used** on this module, so
  the load budget is the non-RF one — ≈0.08–0.12A typical, ≈0.15–0.2A peak
  (ESP32-S3 with RF disabled + ES8388 + MCP6002 + LED). The 600mA AP2112K-3.3
  covers that with ≈3x margin; no redesign required. See `POWER.md` §2/§3 for the
  numbers and the SOT-23-5 thermal note (no exposed pad — a future RF-enabled
  firmware would run hot).

## 1.1.0 — 5V input variant (`32bit-5v`)
New module, derived from `32bit` 1.0.0: the same board with an on-board 3.3V LDO,
so the module can be powered from a 5V slot rail. Nothing else about the module
changes — outline, mounting holes, connector type/pinout, mating geometry and all
existing signal routing are untouched. See `POWER.md` for the rail map, the load
budget and the thermal analysis.

* **Supply socket `V_SUPPLY1` now carries `+5V`** (all five pins), not `+3V3`.
  On a base v1.3.0+ board that is the slot rail with the rail-select jumper fitted
  (`5V_SYS`); **the jumper must be fitted**, because the base's fail-safe default
  is 3.3V and with only 3.3V in the regulator drops out and the module would run
  below 3.0V (authoritative note in `POWER.md`).
* **New `U5` = AP7361C-33E-13** (Diodes Inc., LCSC `C500795`), 1A low-dropout
  linear regulator, SOT-223, 340mV dropout at 1A, 2.2–6.0V input. The SOT-223
  (`-33E`) pinout is 1=IN, 2=GND/tab, 3=OUT, so the exposed tab — and therefore
  the thermal path — is ground. This package has no EN pin, so no EN divider is
  required.
* **New `C40` = 10uF/25V X5R 0805** (LCSC `C15850`) on the regulator input and
  **`C41` = 22uF/25V X5R 0805** (LCSC `C45783`) on its output — the values the
  AP7361C datasheet requires (>=1uF in, >=2.2uF out) with margin, placed on the
  back side within 2mm of the regulator. Value, MPN, Manufacturer, LCSC and
  Datasheet are recorded on both the symbols and the footprints.
* **Original decoupling is unchanged**: `C6`/`C8` on `+3V3`, and through `FB1`
  (ESP32 rail) and `R15` (codec rail) the six 100uF and the codec capacitors. The
  module has its own local bulk, so no extra bulk was added beyond `C40`/`C41`.
* **Copper**: 0.8mm `+5V` from socket pin 4 to the regulator input; the two old
  `+3V3` feeds out of `V_SUPPLY1` were removed and the regulator output now feeds
  both the original `+3V3` via at (52.578, 50.038) (FB1/C8/R3) and the original
  `In2.Cu` `+3V3` distribution trunk, so every original load is still fed.
* **New bottom-side parts.** The top side has no room for a SOT-223 plus two
  0805s (checked with a 0.25mm-grid free-space search), so `U5`, `C40` and `C41`
  are placed on the back side in the free area next to `V_SUPPLY1`. The module's
  underside keep-out is unchanged: the parts are 1.8mm tall against the 4.4mm of
  clearance the base documents, so the mating height is unaffected.
* **Verification** (KiCad 10.0.6, see `verification/`): ERC 55 -> 54 findings,
  **zero new findings** (the `+3V3` net gained a power output, which removed one
  `power_pin_not_driven`); DRC 102 -> 105 with **0 unconnected items** and
  schematic parity 62 -> 4, and the only new findings are three
  `lib_footprint_mismatch` warnings for the three new footprints (same class as
  the 53 pre-existing ones: their bottom-side silkscreen was stripped and their
  fields hidden to avoid ~40 silkscreen/text-height violations). Netlist proof:
  the `+5V` net is exactly {`V_SUPPLY1`.1-5, `U5`.IN, `C40`.1} and every load the
  original `+3V3` net drove is still driven (15/15 acceptance checks pass).
* **Production outputs regenerated and renamed** (`production/32bit-5v.zip`,
  `bom.csv`, `positions.csv`, `designators.csv`, `netlist.ipc`); no exported file
  carries the old `32bit` name. The stale `production/backups/` snapshots of the
  parent module were intentionally not carried over.
* **Deliberately unchanged**: the `32BIT` / `32BIT 0.3.0` front silkscreen (no
  new silkscreen findings) and `code/32bit` (the firmware is identical for both
  variants — same MCU, same pinout — and renaming it would break its build).
* Source module: `modules/32bit` 1.0.0, left byte-identical.

